{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, shorten only the causal pooling half-life from three interactions to two, retaining the incumbent 0.8*h_t+0.2*m_t readout. This tests whether faster decay reduces stale-interest interference while retaining the benefit of pooled states. Exclude padding and future states; preserve final-real-position inference, truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer. Synchronize predictor writer and checkpoint metadata.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, add a fixed prefix-maturity input feature by multiplying each real history embedding by 1+0.05*min(log2(1+k)/4,1), where k counts observed real interactions through that position within the retained prefix. This mildly distinguishes sparse from established context without repeat-count or popularity features. Keep padding zero, candidate embeddings, learned embedding design, GRU topology, readout, loss and optimizer fixed. Reproduce the causal feature in the predictor writer.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, add a fixed residual path inside the recurrent encoder: replace each raw GRU output h_t with h_t+0.1*e_t, using the existing undropped, jointly trained item embedding e_t before the unchanged causal pooling readout. This may preserve recent-item identity alongside recurrent context. Retain the GRU backbone, input features, embedding parameterization, sequence selection, pooling, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer. Update the predictor writer and architecture metadata.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, change only the pairwise term from softplus(s_neg-s_pos) to softplus(0.2+s_neg-s_pos), retaining the incumbent 0.2 pointwise-loss coefficient and valid-position averaging. A modest positive ranking margin may improve separation without repeating unsuccessful pointwise-weight changes. Preserve features, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, optimizer, negative sampling, scheduler and batch/epoch settings; retain one independently trained checkpoint per category."
}
